/* =============================================================================
   'RolePreference Eligibilities' — the Name column, and how to add rows
   =============================================================================
   WHY "NAME" IS READ-ONLY, AND WHY IT IS NOT A PERSON

   Two separate things are going on:

   1. There is no relationship between 'RolePreference Eligibilities' and
      'RolePreference People'. The only link to a person is the EmployeeID TEXT
      column. Nothing will ever resolve a name here, because there is nothing
      for it to resolve against.

   2. The column headed "Name" is the table's PRIMARY NAME column, and it is an
      AUTONUMBER column - values 1000, 1001, ... (1000 is Dataverse's default
      autonumber seed). Autonumber columns are system-generated: read-only in
      the grid, and NOT writable by Patch.

   Confirm (2) if you want: table designer -> the Name column -> Data type. If
   it says Autonumber, everything below applies.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ADDING A ROW
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Leave Name alone. It is required, but Dataverse fills it on save - that is
   why you cannot type into it.

   Fill EmployeeID and RoleKey and save. Those are the only two columns the app
   reads; Area and Grade are carried from the import for reference only.

     - EmployeeID must match 'RolePreference People'.EmployeeID exactly, or
       that person cannot use the app at all.
     - RoleKey must match 'RolePreference Roles'.RoleKey exactly, or the option
       shows as "[missing role: ...]" - see diagnose-missing-roles.powerfx.

   One row per option. Add a row to grant an option, delete a row to remove one.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   FINDING SOMEONE'S ROWS
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Look the person up in 'RolePreference People' first, note the EmployeeID,
   then filter 'RolePreference Eligibilities' on it. You cannot search this
   table by name.

   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   IF YOU WANT TO SEARCH THIS TABLE BY NAME (optional)
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------
   CORRECTION: an earlier version of this file tried to Patch the person's name
   into the Name column. That cannot work against an autonumber column - the
   Patch is rejected. Add a separate column instead:

   1. Table designer -> New column -> name it PersonName, type Text, length 200.
   2. Temporary button on any screen -> OnSelect -> the block below.
   3. Preview, press once, wait for the banner, then DELETE the button.
   4. Add PersonName to the table view so you can sort and search on it.

   This is cosmetic. The app never reads PersonName, and it is a snapshot - if
   somebody's name changes in People, re-run it.

   It patches every row individually, so with ~453 rows give it a minute or two
   and leave the screen open until the banner appears.

   Check what you are about to touch first:
     CountRows('RolePreference Eligibilities') & " rows will be labelled"
   ============================================================================ */

ForAll(
    'RolePreference Eligibilities' As e,
    Patch(
        'RolePreference Eligibilities',
        e,
        {PersonName: Coalesce(LookUp('RolePreference People', EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID).Name, "UNKNOWN PERSON")}
    )
);
Notify("Labelled " & CountRows('RolePreference Eligibilities') & " eligibility rows. Delete this button.", NotificationType.Success)

/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Rows that come out as "UNKNOWN PERSON" have an EmployeeID that is not in
   People. Those people cannot use the app at all - they get "your account is
   not set up for this app yet" - so they are worth finding before UAT does.
   Temporary label:

     CountRows(
         Filter(
             'RolePreference Eligibilities' As e,
             Not IsBlank(e.EmployeeID) And IsBlank(LookUp('RolePreference People', EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID).Name)
         )
     ) & " eligibility rows have an EmployeeID that is not in People"
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
